<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Captaincy General of Cuba</h1><p class="st-meta">1511 CE – 1898 CE · capital Havana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Spain's largest Caribbean colony, whose port of Havana anchored the treasure fleets and whose sugar economy became the richest in the world.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez-de-cuellar">Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar</a> led the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> from 1511, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baracoa">Baracoa</a> and other towns; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatuey">Hatuey</a>, who resisted, was burnt in 1512. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>, moved to its present site around 1519, became the island's main city and, from 1607, its capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> was the gathering port of the treasure fleets returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and was heavily fortified. The British occupied it in 1762-63. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a> of the 1790s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> became the world's leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> producer, relying on hundreds of thousands of enslaved Africans; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> lasted until 1886. Captains-general such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-tacon">Miguel Tacón</a> ruled with broad powers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-years-war">Ten Years' War</a> of 1868-78, led by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-cuba-in-arms">Republic of Cuba in Arms</a>, and the war of 1895 challenged Spanish rule. After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> intervention in 1898, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> gave up the island, which passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-states-military-government-in-cuba">United States Military Government in Cuba</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/captaincy-general-of-cuba.jpg" alt="Captaincy General of Cuba" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: previous version User:Ignaciogavira ; current version HansenBCN, designs from SanchoPanzaXXI · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Spain_(1785%E2%80%931873,_1875%E2%80%931931).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1511 CE – 1898 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Havana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar, Miguel Tacón, Valeriano Weyler</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Cuba</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Spanish Cuba, Island of Cuba</dd></dl></aside></div>
